WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.260
[Music]

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:30.440
[Woman writhes uncomfortably in hospital bed, moaning.]

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:36.440
[Carol Winney:] Jerry. Don't worry, dear.
I'll be all right.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.860
What about the kids? Who's with them?

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.560
[Jerry Winney:] They're okay, honey. Sally's
taking care of them.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.770
[Carol:] You should be with them, dear.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:48.980
They're so young, and they'll imagine all sorts of...

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.900
Why don't you go home for dinner? They'll
take good care of me here.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.720
[Dr. Simmons:] Carol's right, Jerry. The children need some reassurance now.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.210
There'll be a nurse on duty in this room around the clock,

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:04.140
myself or another doctor within call. You
better go home.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.600
[Jerry:] All right, but I'll be back
first thing in the morning.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.300
I love you, honey.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.780
[Jerry kisses Carol goodbye.]

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:19.580
Bye.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:41.740
[Jerry and Dr. Simmons leave the hospital
room as a nurse walks in and over to Carol's bed.]

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:47.679
[Jerry:] Dr. Simmons, what is this with Carol? How bad is she?

00:01:47.679 --> 00:01:52.299
You've known us for years. I want you to give
it to me straight. What's happening to her?

00:01:52.299 --> 00:02:01.859
[Dr. Simmons:] I'll be frank with you, Jerry.
She has tetanus, a highly dangerous disease.

00:02:01.859 --> 00:02:05.060
She's going to be a very sick girl.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:10.289
Right now, I can't honestly tell you how sick,
but be prepared for this.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:11.790
She's going to have a rough time.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:17.040
At the very best, she'll be in the hospital
for weeks and weeks.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.480
Now you take good care of the youngsters,
and we'll let you know if there's any change here.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.170
[Jerry:] Well, thank you, doctor. I'll
be back in the morning.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.340
I know you'll do everything, I mean...

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.019
[Dr. Simmons:] Of course, we will, Jerry.
Everything possible.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:38.170
[Jerry:] What I can't figure, doctor,
why Carol wasn't immunized for this thing?

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:43.040
[Dr. Simmons:] Jerry, I don't know how many
times I've urged her to be immunized.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.700
She always put it off.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.420
"No time this week." That sort of thing. And
now.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.780
[Jerry:] Now is the payoff.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.800
[The doctor shakes his head worriedly.]

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.760
See you tomorrow, doctor.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.560
[Music]

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.020
[Jerry is seated with his children at home.]

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:10.200
[Jerry:] You see, the hospital's the best place. Mom will have everything she needs there.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:16.319
Special nurses and all. And we'll make out
until she feels better.

00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:22.790
[Mike Winney:] But, Dad, Mom must feel real
sick because she never gives in.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:29.980
[Jerry:] Yes, Mike. She is sick. And
you're right, she has courage.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.680
That's why I know she'll be home again in
a few weeks.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.260
Until then, we'll just have to run the house.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.520
[Mike:] We'll do all we can to help,
Dad, won't we, Jenny.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.560
[Jenny Winney:] Sure, Dad. I'll do the dishes
every night. You'll see.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.920
[Jerry:] I know you will. Well, give
me a kiss and get to bed.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:50.940
Tomorrow's a school day, you know.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.960
[Music]

00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:59.560
[Mike:] Dad? Mom will come home, won't she?

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:04.849
[Jerry:] She has to, son. She just has to.

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:10.849
[Mike:] We'll say our prayers for her
tonight, Dad. She'll be all right.

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:14.160
[Music]

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.280
[Jerry, thinking:] If she'd just gotten her shots.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:26.720
When I think back on how it happened, it just doesn't seem possible.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.220
[Carol is gardening.]

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:40.440
[She seems to injure her finger.]

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.400
[She goes inside and bandages the finger.]

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.680
How could a little scratch like that cause
so much trouble?

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.100
She didn't even say anything about it that day.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.800
Everything went along the same as usual.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.010
[Carol:] Jerry, how are the hamburgers coming?

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.650
The kids will be here any minute.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:12.140
[Jerry:] Coming up. Onions, pickle
relish, chili sauce, the works.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:16.300
I estimated six for Mike. Do you think that'll
hold him?

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.840
Ah, here come the troops now.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.110
[Mike:] Hey, Dad. You should have seen
the scrimmage.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:27.420
I completed four passes. The last one was
about fifty yards.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.380
Well, thirty anyway.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.820
[Jerry:] Well, now that's great, champ.
I'm sorry I got home too late to be there.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.420
Sit down. Dinner's ready.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.100
[Mike:] Hey, Mom. Could you wash my
uniform so it will be clean for the game tomorrow?

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:42.160
[Carol:] Sure, Mike. I'll do it.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.280
[Jerry:] I don't know what we'd do
without her.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.780
She holds the whole family together.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:54.330
You never think about something you have until
you don't have it anymore.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:57.770
How long was it before she started feeling
bad?

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.740
It all happened so fast.

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:01.160
[Carol is making a phone call.]

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.360
[Carol:] Jerry?

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.100
[Jerry:] Hi, Carol. What are you doing?
I thought you were playing bridge.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.010
[Carol:] Well, I'm not feeling so good.
That's why I called.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.740
[Jerry:] Well, what's wrong?

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:16.470
[Carol:] Oh, I feel kind of feverish
and have this stiffness in all my muscles,

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:23.050
and then, well, I'm weak all over, and I have
a splitting headache.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:28.380
Another thing, I don't exactly know how to
explain it, but, well, I'm so restless or something.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.210
Well, I bawled the kids out this morning,

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.790
and they really didn't do anything wrong.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:37.520
[Jerry:] Well, maybe you ought to give
the doctor a call.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.450
[Carol:] You think so, too? Well, then
I'll call Dr. Simmons.

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:47.610
It's probably nothing serious, but, well,
I'd feel better anyway.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.750
[Jerry:] Listen, let me know as soon
as you find out something, will you?

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.750
[Carol:] OK. I'll call and let you know. Bye, sweetie.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:07:00.520
[Jerry hangs up the phone, looking concerned.]

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:07.740
[Dr. Simmons:] Carol, you know I'm not disposed
to be an alarmist. Quite the contrary.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:09.580
But after what you told me on the phone,

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:12.810
I felt it my duty to order you down here on
the double.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.910
Some things you can't let go.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:22.050
Now, try and remember just when it was that
you got this scratch, and how.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:27.440
[Carol:] Let's see. It must have been,
yes, Tuesday,

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:28.810
in the middle of the morning.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.530
I was gardening, and something in the mulch,

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:36.400
I don't know what it was, nicked my finger
a little, and that's all.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.780
[Dr. Simmons:] In the mulch? So your hands were dirty?

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:43.180
[Carol:] Well, you can't garden and
keep snow-white hands, you know.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.500
[Dr. Simmons:] Yes, I know. My wife has a
green thumb, too.

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:54.000
Carol, you never got those tetanus immunizations
we talked about, did you?

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.540
[Carol:] No, I just never did get around
to it.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:01.510
Do you suppose that might be what's wrong?

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:06.700
[Dr. Simmons:] Well, I don't say that it is,
but it could very well be.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:11.440
We're going to admit you to the hospital for
observation and further testing right now.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:18.520
[Music]

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:22.960
[Jerry, thinking:] What a shock it was when the doctor told me.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:26.540
Tetanus. Lockjaw.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:30.420
Something you never even think about.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.460
[Phone rings.]

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:43.660
[Jerry:] Hello. Oh, yes, Sally.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.400
Thanks so much for staying with the kids today.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.720
No, I don't know yet.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:54.020
But I'm going over first thing in the morning.

00:08:54.020 --> 00:09:00.390
Oh, I surely will, and thanks for calling.
Bye.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:06.760
[He hangs up the phone slowly.]

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:07.580
[Jerry is at the hospital.]

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:09.020
[Jerry:] Good morning. Is Dr. Simmons in?

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:12.260
[Nurse:] Dr. Simmons is in with your wife
now, Mr. Winney.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:14.340
Well, why don't you step down the hall and
wait for him?

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.970
[Jerry:] Thank you.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.410
[Nurse:] Oh, here he comes now.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.920
[Jerry:] Morning, doctor. How's Carol
this morning?

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.960
[Dr. Simmons:] About as expected.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:33.160
Jerry, I told you it was going to be a long,
tough fight.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:36.170
She's going to get worse before she gets better.

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:37.840
We have to face that.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:39.830
We've had one good break, though.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:43.120
She's shown no adverse reaction to tetanus anti-toxins,

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:48.120
so we've been able to give her large doses
of that. It should help.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:50.230
[Jerry:] Can I see her?

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:52.330
[Dr. Simmons:] Well, yes, but, Jerry.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.380
[Jerry:] Yes?

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.130
[Dr. Simmons:] It's not pretty, and be very
quiet.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:00.000
Any little noise or vibration could cause
a spasm.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.080
[Jerry:] Oh. Will she know who I am?

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:03.240
[Dr. Simmons:] Oh, yes. She's conscious.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.330
Sedated, but she'll know you.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:10.070
[Music]

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:18.760
[Jerry and Dr. Simmons walk into the hospital room and stand near Carol.]

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:30.560
[Carol is moaning in her hospital bed.]

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:36.080
[She grimaces in pain, her jaw locked.]

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:40.600
[Jerry peers down at Carol, disturbed by her distress.]

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:52.100
[Carol writhes in pain, arching her neck and back.]

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:53.400
[Jerry:] Doctor, can't you do anything?

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:57.440
[Dr. Simmons:] Jerry, we're doing everything we can.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:59.330
She's trying to get your attention.

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:11.370
[Carol tries to speak.]

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:16.120
[Jerry leans over her bed, trying to understand her.]

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:21.280
[Music]

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:32.460
[Jerry and the doctor leave the room.]

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:36.900
[A nurse presses cool cloths on Carol's face.]

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:41.400
[Jerry:] Lockjaw. Why does she have
to suffer?

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.530
[Dr. Simmons:] Well, you have every right
to be upset, Jerry,

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:48.340
but this can happen to anybody who isn't protected,

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:50.680
and the suffering goes with it.

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:54.860
Let's find a seat, and I'll give you some
background

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:58.150
so you'll have a better picture of what's
going on.

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:06.070
[The two walk over a pair of armchairs and sit down.]

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:12.380
Jerry, tetanus bacteria grow in the intestines of grass-eating animals,

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.420
such as horses or cows.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:22.190
So, as you may guess, they're frequently found around barnyards and fields used for grazing,

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:25.890
but this doesn't mean they're just way out
in the country far from city life.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:29.320
There are dairies in and around every city
in the United States,

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.940
and that means cows.

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:35.529
There are riding stables, park areas, zoos,

00:12:35.529 --> 00:12:40.270
many different activities involving grass-eating
animals wherever you are.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:46.020
Further, and this is even more significant,
when the bacteria in the soil dry up,

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:50.800
tiny spores are formed, each one as deadly
as the bacteria.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:55.900
And they live almost indefinitely.

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.270
These spores can go anywhere, carried by the
wind,

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:02.160
and lie in wait for a victim,

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.779
and they're not just outdoors.

00:13:04.779 --> 00:13:12.130
They're brought in to homes, churches, schools,
everywhere.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:13.500
On shoes, clothing.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:19.140
It's safe to say that tetanus spores may be
found in any room you enter.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:22.250
Of course, the spores may be concentrated

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:28.230
wherever manure is used to fertilize trees,
shrubs, and flowers.

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:32.250
That's probably the source of Carol's infection.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:36.820
We fear the spores or bacteria most in dirty
wounds,

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:39.520
such as punctures from nails, or gunshots...

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:41.320
wounds from automobile accidents,

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:45.160
but they can enter the body through any break
in the skin,

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:47.230
no matter how superficial.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:50.600
Over fifty percent of all cases come from
wounds so trivial

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:53.040
that people don't even seek medical attention,

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:57.250
and usually involve an abrasion which is contaminated
with soil,

00:13:57.250 --> 00:14:02.010
such as the scratch on Carol's finger.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:04.770
Although everybody's exposed to tetanus

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.860
because everybody gets a scratch occasionally,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:12.830
certain types of occupations are more frequently
exposed.

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.560
For example, construction workers and fieldhands,

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:16.830
outdoor laborers,

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:21.490
industrial workers in plants and factories
who work with their hands.

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:23.670
And then there are children.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:25.980
You know how active they are.

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:30.149
Always falling down, scraping a knee or a
hand.

00:14:30.149 --> 00:14:33.270
Recently, women have become more and more
exposed, too.

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:35.010
You see, more than ever,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:39.440
they're participating in sports, going camping,
working in gardens.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.279
They need protection just as much as men do.

00:14:43.279 --> 00:14:48.870
Once inside the body, the germs produce a
powerful nerve toxin or poison

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:53.440
that causes painful convulsions or spasms
of the voluntary muscles.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:57.990
One of the most frequently affected muscles
is the jaw muscle,

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:02.460
causing the patient's jaw to close tightly.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:07.260
This characteristic symptom gave rise to the
common or slang name for tetanus,

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.660
lockjaw.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:16.040
Over half the infected people die from the
disease, unless treated quickly.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:21.120
I hope we've caught Carol's in time.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:25.290
The terrible thing about tetanus is that it's
absolutely unnecessary.

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:27.620
It should never happen.

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:33.200
Yet we have hundreds of cases each year in
this country.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:34.730
Remember when you were in the service,

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:36.430
and you got your tetanus immunization.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:40.520
Well, tetanus vaccine is, for all practical
purposes,

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:42.540
one hundred percent effective,

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:45.920
and there's virtually no serious reaction.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:51.350
Of course, you should have booster immunizations
at regular intervals.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:57.279
There are no documented cases of immunized
persons dying from tetanus.

00:15:57.279 --> 00:16:00.600
Most men get their immunization in the service.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.830
So a big problem is with women.

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:05.510
They should be immunized, too.

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:12.870
For the past thirty years or so, immunizations
have been given to infants

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:16.060
in combination with diphtheria and whooping
cough.

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:19.950
But don't worry, Mike and Jenny have had theirs.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:22.399
But there are millions of children under five

00:16:22.399 --> 00:16:24.550
who have never had this protection.

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:26.000
They need immunization.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:29.940
Another thing, tetanus is no respecter of
age either.

00:16:29.940 --> 00:16:33.880
It strikes anytime from birth to old age.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:36.180
There are millions of elderly people in the
United States

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.630
who've never been immunized and should be.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:42.920
There would be no tetanus in the United States
at all

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:46.230
if everyone would get their immunizations.

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:52.120
But that's water over the dam for Carol.

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:55.200
If she pulls through this time, we'll make
sure she's immunized.

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:58.120
[Jerry:] You mean she could get it
again?

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:03.830
[Dr. Simmons:] Oh, yes. Having this disease
does not confer immunity.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.630
You must have the toxoid.

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:09.579
[Jerry:] You just said over half of
the people die.

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:13.250
What are Carol's chances?

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:19.560
[Dr. Simmons:] That's the truth, Jerry. Carol
has a 50/50 chance.

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:22.569
Maybe a little better.

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:25.360
Her symptoms have progressed rather slowly.

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:28.549
That's on the good side.

00:17:28.549 --> 00:17:31.690
Let's just say she's holding her own right
now.

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:38.389
So if there's no change tonight, there's no
use you sitting around here.

00:17:38.389 --> 00:17:40.739
Much better you be home with the kids.

00:17:40.739 --> 00:17:45.129
If you are needed, I'll call you.

00:17:48.520 --> 00:18:04.020
[Jerry walks through the door of his home and looks around at objects that remind him of Carol...

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:19.380
a sweater, a sewing machine, a photograph, a bouquet of flowers, which he touches.]

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:38.380
[Jerry walks over to a chair, sits down, and throws his head back.]

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:41.619
[Jerry, thinking:] What would I do without her?

00:18:41.619 --> 00:18:43.320
What would the children do?

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:48.600
She just can't. She just can't.

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:50.980
[Jenny:] Dad! Dad!

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:55.780
[Jerry:] Hmm? Oh, yes. What do you want,
honey?

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:59.120
[Jenny:] Well, Mom always reads my
spelling words to me before a test.

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:01.300
Could you do it for me, Dad?

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:04.060
[Jerry:] Why certainly. Let me see
the book.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:10.160
You ready?

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:11.920
Uh, necessary.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:16.660
[Jenny:] N-E-C-E-S-S-A-R-Y.

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:20.700
[Jerry:] Right. Habitation.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:24.040
[Jenny:] H-A-B-I-T-A-T-I-O-N.

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:26.021
[Phone ringing.]

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:31.060
[Jerry runs to the phone and the children watch him.]

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:35.360
[Jerry:] Hello. Yes.

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:41.760
Yes, of course. I'll be there right away.

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:45.420
Mike, I've got to go to the hospital for a
little while.

00:19:45.429 --> 00:19:47.519
You go next door and tell Mrs. Barnes, will
you?

00:19:47.519 --> 00:19:48.970
She'll come over and stay with you,

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:51.029
And I'll see you in the morning.

00:19:51.029 --> 00:19:52.850
[Mike:] What's the matter, Dad?

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:55.879
[Jerry:] They just want to talk to
me, son.

00:19:55.879 --> 00:19:57.119
You mind Mrs. Barnes now.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:58.580
[Mike:] OK, Dad.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:03.820
[Dr. Simmons:] You got here in a hurry, Jerry.

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:05.460
Sit down for a moment, go ahead.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:06.840
[Jerry:] Oh, I'm all right. Well, what's
wrong?

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:08.100
Is she worse?

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.609
[Dr. Simmons:] Well, she's had laryngeal spasms.

00:20:11.609 --> 00:20:14.590
That means closing off her windpipe,

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.299
which could stop her breathing.

00:20:17.299 --> 00:20:20.309
We've had to insert a tube in her windpipe.

00:20:20.309 --> 00:20:23.109
It's one of the hazards of tetanus.

00:20:23.109 --> 00:20:25.859
[Jerry:] What are her chances now?

00:20:25.859 --> 00:20:29.639
[Dr. Simmons:] Well, the odds have certainly
gotten worse.

00:20:29.639 --> 00:20:31.850
It's not hopeless,

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:37.020
but it's not a good situation.

00:20:37.029 --> 00:20:38.159
If we can pull her through tonight,

00:20:38.159 --> 00:20:40.470
I'd say she has a good chance then.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:43.309
[Jerry:] Can I see her?

00:20:43.309 --> 00:20:51.509
[Dr. Simmons:] If you make it brief. She's
heavily sedated. She may not know you this time.

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:56.600
[The men walk toward Carol's room.]

00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:03.540
[Medical staff are bent over Carol's bed. Carol's breathing is labored.]

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:10.480
[Jerry pauses and looks at Carol, then approaches the bed.]

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:32.280
[Jerry stands next to her bed. Carol's eyes
are covered and a tube extends from her throat.]

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:41.820
[Moments pass, Jerry appearing deeply worried.]

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:55.380
[Jerry and Dr. Simmons turn to go, and the medical staff return to Carol's side.]

00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:03.000
[Jerry sits in a chair, considering.]

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:07.080
[Jerry, thinking:] She'll get through the night.

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:10.370
She'll make it all right.

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:12.250
Things will be like they always were.

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:13.570
[The family is gathered together at home.]

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:19.560
[Carol, Mike, and Jenny Winney:] Happy birthday to you, happy birthday to you.

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:24.440
Happy birthday, dear Daddy.

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:28.389
[Winney family:] Happy birthday to you, me.

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:34.920
[Carol puts drops in Mike's nose.]

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:40.740
[Carol trims Jenny's hair in the kitchen.]

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:45.700
[Carol reads with Mike.]

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:53.160
[Carol is shown lying in bed with her eye
bandages.]

00:22:56.040 --> 00:22:59.240
[Jerry, thinking:] She's not going to make it.

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:01.899
There's no sense in kidding myself.

00:23:01.899 --> 00:23:04.259
She's not gonna make it.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:09.300
[Jerry's head lolls back and his eyes close; he seems to have dozed off.]

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:14.140
[Music]

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:32.379
[Jerry is dreaming, seeing filmy images of
nurses and brightly lit hospital rooms.]

00:23:32.379 --> 00:23:46.979
[He imagines nurses and other medical staff, beckoning him down corridors and towards Carol's room.]

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:52.560
[Everything is in slow-motion. Jerry and others walk down the corridor.]

00:23:52.560 --> 00:24:02.760
[A nurse gestures from the nurse's station. then emerges and signals Jerry to follow her.]

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:12.540
[Entering Carol's hospital room, Jerry sees that the bed is empty, neatly made up.]

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:18.500
[Menacing music.]

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:23.920
[A hand comes toward Jerry's face.]

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:25.120
[Dr. Simmons:] Jerry, come out of it.

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:26.200
[Jerry:] She's gone.

00:24:29.560 --> 00:24:33.680
[Jerry comes to.]

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:35.460
[Jerry:] What's the matter?

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:37.160
[Dr. Simmons:] You're exhausted.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:38.860
That's what's the matter.

00:24:38.869 --> 00:24:43.999
Three days of no sleep finally caught up
with you. It's morning.

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:49.960
[Jerry:] Morning? How's Carol?

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:51.739
[Dr. Simmons:] Good news.

00:24:51.739 --> 00:24:53.539
Her condition improved during the night.

00:24:53.539 --> 00:24:56.179
She's going to pull through. We feel sure.

00:24:56.179 --> 00:24:57.759
Why don't you go call the kids?

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:01.059
They're scared, too, and then come on back here.

00:25:01.060 --> 00:25:04.340
[Jerry:] OK. I'll call. She's going to be all right?

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:05.940
[Dr. Simmons nods yes.]

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:14.360
Oh, man. What a dream. She's coming home.

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:18.880
[Phone rings; Mike Winney dashes to answer it.]

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:20.759
[Mike:] Hello. Hi, Dad.

00:25:22.729 --> 00:25:28.769
She is? Sure. Sure. Bye.

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:30.400
Mom's going to be all right.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:31.860
Dad just said so.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.090
She'll come home.

00:25:36.259 --> 00:25:40.609
[Dr. Simmons:] Well, what did the kids think
about the good news?

00:25:40.609 --> 00:25:43.119
[Jerry:] I talked to Mike, and he was
really pleased.

00:25:43.119 --> 00:25:44.229
[Dr. Simmons:] Good, good.

00:25:44.229 --> 00:25:46.159
[Jerry:] Can I see Carol now?

00:25:46.159 --> 00:25:50.340
[Dr. Simmons:] Well, it would be best if you
can wait until this afternoon to see her, Jerry.

00:25:50.629 --> 00:25:54.799
She's still a mighty sick girl and will be
for several days.

00:25:54.799 --> 00:25:58.190
She'll stay here in the hospital for quite
a while.

00:25:58.190 --> 00:26:02.830
Those stiff, rigid muscles and spasms will
persist,

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:06.970
but they'll gradually get fewer and further
apart until she's completely recovered.

00:26:06.970 --> 00:26:11.570
I can tell you this. We were lucky, mighty
lucky.

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:13.979
Tetanus is something doctors dread.

00:26:13.979 --> 00:26:18.259
As I told you before, even with prompt round-the-clock
treatment,

00:26:18.259 --> 00:26:21.600
we still lose about 50 percent of the cases.

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:26.809
And without treatment, death is virtually
certain.

00:26:26.809 --> 00:26:32.440
[Jerry:] I just never knew. If people
only realized, this sort of thing wouldn't happen.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:33.649
[Dr. Simmons:] Right.

00:26:34.049 --> 00:26:35.989
[Jerry:] I won't keep you anymore.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:40.020
Maybe I ought to get some breakfast in me.

00:26:40.029 --> 00:26:42.859
[Dr. Simmons:] That's a good idea. I'm going
back to Carol.

00:26:42.859 --> 00:26:46.590
I'll see you this afternoon.

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:56.190
[The doctor stands over Carol's bed.]

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:59.389
So unnecessary.

00:27:02.380 --> 00:27:06.560
There wouldn't be a case of tetanus in this
country ever,

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:12.730
if people would just get immunized.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:29.930
[Music]

